  "root_cause": {
    "hypothesis": "The kernel indexes row-major A with a column-major stride (`A[k*M + row]`) instead of `A[row*K + k]`, so it multiplies A^T by B and every C element is wrong.",
    "mechanism": "Host allocates A as MxK row-major and the CPU reference uses A[m*K + k]. The kernel's `A[k*M + row]` treats A as if it were column-major, reading transposed elements. For each (row,col), the accumulated dot product is sum_k A[k,row]*B[k,col] instead of sum_k A[row,k]*B[k,col], so every C[row,col] disagrees with the reference \u2014 max_abs_err in the thousands. As a secondary effect, since the A index doesn't depend on threadIdx.x (col), neighboring threads in a wave hit the same/scattered addresses instead of contiguous ones, defeating coalescing. Fixing the index to A[row*K + k] restores correctness and yields contiguous loads across k for a fixed row.",
    "confidence": 0.98,
    "bug_type": "uncoalesced_access",
    "affected_lines": [
      54
    ]
  },
  "patch_plan": {
    "summary": "Change A index from column-major `A[k*M + row]` to row-major `A[row*K + k]`.",
    "diff": "@@\n-        float a = A[k * M + row];\n+        float a = A[row * K + k];\n         float b = B[k * N + col];",
    "risks": [
      "None for M=N=K=256 (all indices remain in bounds); no ABI/API change."
    ],
    "expected_effect": "MAX_ABS_ERR ~ 0 and STATUS: PASS; also improves throughput due to coalesced A loads.",
    "validation_steps": [
      "hipcc -O2 -std=c++17 gemm_uncoalesced.hip -o gemm_uncoalesced",
      "./gemm_uncoalesced",
      "check STATUS: PASS and MAX_ABS_ERR ~ 0"
    ]
  },
